Kubernetes real time scenarios based questions and answers

🚨 Scenario 1: Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff
Q: A pod keeps restarting and shows CrashLoopBackOff. What will you do?
Check pod status
kubectl get pods
Describe the pod (events are gold)
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Check container logs

kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>
If app exits immediately, check:
Wrong command/args
Missing env variables
ConfigMap/Secret issues
If needed, increase restart delay for debugging:
restartPolicy: Never

🚨 Scenario 2: Pod stuck in Pending
Q: A pod is not starting and stuck in Pending. Why?
A:Common causes:
No nodes with enough CPU/memory
Node selector / taints & tolerations mismatch
PVC not bound
Debug steps:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Typical messages:
0/5 nodes available: insufficient memory
pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims
Fix:Increase cluster capacity
Fix resource requests
Create PV or use correct StorageClass

🚨 Scenario 3: Service is not accessible

Q: Pods are running but Service is not reachable.
A:Checklist:
Service selector matches pod labels?

kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc <service-name>
Pod labels:

kubectl get pods --show-labels
Check endpoints:

kubectl get endpoints <service-name>
No endpoints = selector mismatch
Real-world issue:
Typo in label app: web vs app: web-app

🚨 Scenario 4: Application works inside pod but not from outside

Q: App responds inside pod but not externally.

Check Service type:
ClusterIP → internal only
NodePort / LoadBalancer → external
If using Ingress:
Ingress Controller running?
Correct host/path?

kubectl get ingress
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>
Very common miss: DNS / host file not updated for Ingress hostname.

🚨 Scenario 5: High CPU / Memory usage

Q: Pods are getting OOMKilled.
A:Check limits:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Look for:
Last State: Terminated
Reason: OOMKilled

🚨 Scenario 6: Rolling update causes downtime

Q: During deployment update, users see downtime.
A:Check deployment strategy:
strategy:
 type: RollingUpdate
 rollingUpdate:
 maxUnavailable: 0
 maxSurge: 1

🚨 Scenario 7: ConfigMap change not reflected in pods
Q: You updated a ConfigMap but app still uses old config.
A:ConfigMaps are not hot-reloaded unless:
App supports reload
Or pod is restarted
Fix: kubectl rollout restart deployment <deployment-name>
